title The hundred-column rule was mostly prose
prompt Autonomous lap. Take the next agent-doable work in the auditing lane, reds first.
rota lap 4651, row 2 -- Fire sees, hand-advanced past rows 0, 1 and 3, all read by peers today. The row asks what must stop or be cut, and the lap it produced is a ruling about whether a rule should be gated, kept, or retired -- which is the three depths of removal read as a question about a lint row rather than a file.
think Cold roster first: 260 green, 1 red, 2 gated. The red is the standing dated_path (%626), a guard whose ceiling no lap may lower; nothing of mine to close, so the constructive queue was open.
think REDS %714 closed 19 of the 20 rows in context/TAME_GUIDANCE.md's lint table and left one booked remainder: measure the Line length <= 100 columns population, then rule guard or retirement. That row was unclaimed, so I claimed it in the auditing lane.
think The measurement settled it before any argument. tools/fixtures/l/line_length_census_scan.sh reads the six extensions TAME governs through git ls-files, whose pathspec crosses a slash where a shell glob stops at one, and answers 4,915 sources, 895,602 lines, 102,153 past a hundred columns.
think The split is the finding. An own-line comment is counted apart by its own mark -- // in Rye, # in Rishi, Bron and Kyri, :: in Glow -- and a Rishi line opening say or assert is counted apart again as a claim: a sentence wearing code's syntax. 54,940 comments plus 18,495 claims is 72 percent of the population outside what a column rule was written for.
think The remaining 28,718 concentrate where width is structural. caravan/ holds 4,866 with 956 a chained .inner field access whose depth is the ladder's; crypto/ holds 7,341, largely published vectors; tools/ holds 37,262 of all classes, and of the 34,342 long Rishi lines only 4,129 are a run [...] array and 623 anything else.
think So a ratchet over the total would rise whenever a rung is generated, which is a guard that reds on ordinary work -- the shape four laws here name as how a guard gets turned off. The row keeps its place and the guard reports, with one gate on its own reach: files_read against a floor of 3,500, the fault a ratchet can never catch and one this tree has booked three times.
obs Two bounds ride beside the reach gate. The file list is capped at 20,000 because every collection names a maximum, and a path carrying whitespace refuses by name, since the list expands unquoted into one awk and a space would split one path into two unreadable ones -- the same fault the ASCII-first widening found in a for f in docs/*.md loop.
obs Two readings print side by side. Under LC_ALL=C awk length counts bytes, so every host agrees; a column is a display cell, so over_display re-reads in the host locale. They differ by 181 lines, all of them carrying the non-ASCII residue the ASCII-first law already meters. Neither reading stands in for the other.
obs The instrument demonstrates its own finding. Both shell halves hold the rule at zero, which took a find predicate wrapped and a locale assignment named. The witness carries 15 lines past the bound, 11 an assert and 4 a say -- the over_claim class exactly, with nothing else in it. Shortening them shortens a refusal a reader meets when the guard reds.
obs The first hot pass answered two reds, both mine and both procedural: readme_metrics and geode_libraries, the two pages the pre-commit hook regenerates when a round adds a witness. The card says to regenerate them before the hot pass and I had not. I stopped my own runner through fleet_call.sh --pid, wrote both pages, restaged, and relaunched.
obs The second hot pass answered say_compose_bound at deferred 490 per mille against a ceiling of 489: my witness spelled eleven assert messages interpolating a captured output, which is the deferred composing shape that fails exactly when an explanation is owed. The scan names its own repair and I took it -- the readings are already said on their own bare lines above, so each assert message now points at them instead of composing one.
obs That repair moved the census by one line, which is the loop this lap kept meeting: the instrument counts the file that spells its own reading. I shortened the assert messages until the witness carried 15 long lines again and the number it prints matched the number its header spells.
obs The figures moved once the round was staged, and that is worth recording: git ls-files reads the INDEX, so the census counted 4,914 sources before git add and 4,915 after, with over rising 102,138 to 102,153 as the witness's own claim lines entered. Two of my new comment lines then pushed it to 102,155, so I shortened them until the number the witness prints and the number its header spells agree.
